.TH UNEXPAND 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
unexpand \- convert spaces to tabs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unexpand
.RB [ \-af ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR N ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unexpand
copies files to standard output, replacing runs of spaces with tabs
that reach the same columns.
Tab stops are every 8 columns, or every
.I N
columns with
.BR \-t .
It reads each
.I file
in order, and reads standard input when there are no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- .
.BR expand (1)
converts in the other direction.
.PP
A blank is a space or a tab.
Other white space, such as a carriage return, a form feed, or a no-break
space, is ordinary text.
A run of blanks is replaced by one tab for each tab stop that it reaches
or passes, followed by the spaces that reach its last column.
Within a run, a space advances one column and a tab advances to the
next tab stop, so a run may mix them.
A run that reaches no tab stop is copied as spaces, and a single space
is converted when it ends on a tab stop.
.PP
By default, only the first run of blanks in each line is converted: the
leading blanks of a line that starts with a blank, or else the blanks
that follow the first word of the line.
The rest of the line is copied unchanged, including any tabs.
With
.B \-a
or
.BR \-t ,
every run in the line is converted, unless
.B \-f
is also given.
.PP
Columns count from the start of the line.
Text is measured in columns, not bytes.
In UTF-8 text, a wide character such as an ideograph is 2 columns, a
combining character is 0, and an invalid sequence is 1.
In single-byte text, every byte is 1 column; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
Every control character is 1 column, including backspace, carriage
return, and escape.
Backspace does not move the column back, and the bytes of an escape
sequence count.
.PP
A line ends at a newline, which is copied, or at a NUL byte, which is
deleted.
After a NUL byte the count starts over, as if a new line began there.
Line endings are not converted, and a last line without a newline gets
none.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-at4 ,
and the argument of
.B \-t
may be attached, as in
.BR \-t4 ,
or be the next argument.
Long options may be abbreviated to a unique prefix, and the argument of
.B \-\-tabs
may follow
.B =
or be the next argument.
Options may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Convert every run of blanks in each line, not only the first.
Implied by
.BR \-t ,
unless
.B \-f
is given.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-first\-only
Convert only the first run of blanks in each line, as described under
.BR DESCRIPTION .
This is the default.
It overrides
.B \-a
and
.BR \-t ,
wherever they appear on the command line.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-tabs=\fIN\fR
Set tab stops every
.I N
columns.
The default is 8.
.I N
is a decimal integer from 1 to 4294967295, with no sign, prefix, or
suffix, and a leading zero does not make it octal.
It is a single number, not a list.
Any other value is reported as
.B invalid number
or
.BR "not in 1..4294967295 range" ,
with exit status 1, before any input is read.
Giving
.B \-t
also gives
.BR \-a ,
and
.B \-f
overrides both.
If
.B \-t
is repeated, the last one applies.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
An unknown option, or a missing option argument, prints the usage
message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B unexpand
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set, even to an empty value, decides how text
is measured.
If its value is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes, each 1 column, and output to a console is not
converted.
Otherwise text is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, and single
bytes when it is not.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, such as an unknown option, a missing option argument, or
an invalid
.BR \-t ;
a
.I file
that could not be opened or read, including a directory; a failure
writing standard output; or running out of memory.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error, beginning with
.BR unexpand: ,
though an option error may begin with the program's path instead.
The remaining files are still processed after one cannot be opened or
read.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert the leading spaces of a line to tabs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq        x\en\(aq | unexpand | cat \-A
^Ix$
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert every run of blanks, here with tab stops every 4 columns:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa   b   c\en\(aq | unexpand \-t 4 | cat \-A
a^Ib^Ic$
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert only the first run in each line, or every run:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq        a        b\en\(aq | unexpand | cat \-A
^Ia        b$
$ printf \(aq        a        b\en\(aq | unexpand \-a | cat \-A
^Ia^I b$
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert indentation to tabs at 4-column stops, writing a new file.
In a line that does not start with a blank, the blanks after the first
word are converted instead:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unexpand \-f \-t 4 main.c >main.new
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make the blanks of a file consistent by expanding every tab and then
converting every run:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ expand notes.txt | unexpand \-a >notes.new
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU unexpand:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
takes one number, not a list of tab stops.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BI \- N
form of
.BR \-t ,
and there is no
.BR \-\-version .
.IP \(bu 2
By default, the first run of blanks after the first word of a line that
does not start with a blank is converted too.
GNU unexpand converts only leading blanks, which is also what the usage
message of this one claims.
.IP \(bu 2
A single space that ends on a tab stop becomes a tab.
GNU unexpand keeps it when a non-blank follows.
.IP \(bu 2
NUL bytes are deleted.
GNU unexpand keeps them.
.PP
A run of blanks that would be converted is lost, spaces and tabs alike,
when it ends in a tab followed by a NUL byte or by the end of a file
with no final newline.
.PP
Files and standard streams are binary, with no CRLF translation.
A carriage return is not a blank, so blanks before the carriage return
of a CRLF line are converted like blanks before any other character.
.PP
The ANSI code page of busybox is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, so there text is UTF-8 unless a locale variable says otherwise,
and file names may contain any Unicode characters.
On older systems it is the system code page.
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, and escape sequences in the input, such as those for
color, take effect.
.PP
Each
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR expand (1)
